    "ex-heavy-ball-optimized-quadratic-spectrum": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-heavy-ball-rate-on-strongly-convex-quadratics",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$, let $0<c\\le L$, and let $A\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ satisfy $A^{\\mathsf T}=A$ and $x^{\\mathsf T}Ax>0$ for every nonzero $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$. An eigenvalue of $A$ is a real number $\\lambda$ such that $Av=\\lambda v$ for some nonzero $v$; assume every eigenvalue lies in $[c,L]$. For a fixed center $w^\\star\\in\\mathbb R^d$, define $F(w)=\\tfrac12(w-w^\\star)^{\\mathsf T}A(w-w^\\star)$. Then $w^\\star$ is the unique minimizer of $F$. Put $\\kappa=L/c$ and define\n\n$$q:=\\frac{\\sqrt L-\\sqrt c}{\\sqrt L+\\sqrt c}=\\frac{\\sqrt\\kappa-1}{\\sqrt\\kappa+1}.$$\n\nHere $\\nabla F(w)=(\\partial_jF(w))_{0\\le j<d}$ is the vector of coordinate partial derivatives.\n\nFor any constant parameters $\\alpha>0$ and $\\beta\\ge0$, finite horizon $T\\ge1$, and initial vector $w_{\\mathrm{init}}\\in\\mathbb R^d$, define the heavy-ball sequence by $w_0=w_1=w_{\\mathrm{init}}$ and\n\n$$w_{t+1}=w_t-\\alpha\\nabla F(w_t)+\\beta(w_t-w_{t-1}),\\qquad 1\\le t<T.$$\n\nFor each scalar $\\lambda\\in[c,L]$, the associated scalar recurrence is $x_{t+1}=(1+\\beta-\\alpha\\lambda)x_t-\\beta x_{t-1}$. Define its characteristic polynomial and root factor by\n\n$$p_\\lambda(r;\\alpha,\\beta):=r^2-(1+\\beta-\\alpha\\lambda)r+\\beta,\\qquad \\rho_\\lambda(\\alpha,\\beta):=\\max\\{|r|:p_\\lambda(r;\\alpha,\\beta)=0\\}.$$\n\nThis factor is the largest modulus of the two characteristic roots in $\\mathbb C$ of the scalar recurrence for eigenvalue $\\lambda$, not a one-step Euclidean norm contraction. The tuned parameters are\n\n$$\\alpha=\\frac{4}{(\\sqrt L+\\sqrt c)^2},\\qquad \\beta=q^2.$$\n\nFor this choice, $\\rho_\\lambda(\\alpha,\\beta)=q$ for every $\\lambda\\in[c,L]$. If $c<L$, then $p_c(r;\\alpha,\\beta)=(r-q)^2$ and $p_L(r;\\alpha,\\beta)=(r+q)^2$; if $c=L$, the only modal polynomial is $r^2$ and its root factor is $0$.\n\nFor gradient descent with step $\\gamma=2/(L+c)$, the modal multipliers are $1-\\gamma\\lambda$ and their largest absolute value over $[c,L]$ is\n\n$$\\rho_{\\mathrm{GD}}=\\frac{L-c}{L+c}=\\frac{\\kappa-1}{\\kappa+1}.$$\n\nThus $\\rho_{\\mathrm{GD}}>q$ when $c<L$, and both factors are $0$ when $c=L$. Moreover, among all constant heavy-ball parameters $\\alpha'>0$ and $\\beta'\\ge0$, the supremum of their modal root factors over $\\lambda\\in[c,L]$ is at least $q$. The tuned parameters therefore minimize that interval worst-case root factor. When $c<L$, the repeated endpoint roots permit a linear polynomial prefactor multiplying $q^t$ or $(-q)^t$; the root factor does not assert a one-step norm contraction by $q$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-heavy-ball-momentum",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$ be an integer and let $F:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuously Fréchet differentiable: at every $w$, $DF(w)$ is a linear first-order approximation with normwise remainder tending to zero, and $w\\mapsto DF(w)$ is continuous. Write its coordinate gradient as $\\nabla F(w)=(\\partial_jF(w))_{0\\le j<d}$. Given an initial vector $w_{\\mathrm{init}}\\in\\mathbb R^d$, a constant step size $\\alpha>0$, a constant momentum parameter $\\beta\\ge0$, and an integer horizon $T\\ge1$, initialize $w_0=w_1=w_{\\mathrm{init}}$. For each integer $t=1,\\ldots,T-1$, set\n\n$$w_{t+1}:=w_t-\\alpha\\nabla F(w_t)+\\beta(w_t-w_{t-1}).$$\n\nOutput $(w_0,w_1,\\ldots,w_T)$. The gradient is evaluated at the current iterate $w_t$.\nWhen $\\beta=0$, every update is the fixed-step gradient-descent update with step size $\\alpha$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "alg-gradient-descent",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be open and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuously\nFréchet differentiable. The gradient $\\nabla f(x)$ is the vector of partial\nderivatives in the Euclidean coordinates, with the convention of\nThe Jacobian matrix of partial derivatives and the gradient in the scalar-valued case; differentiability is the Fréchet\ncondition of The real Frechet derivative on real and complex matrix spaces with the Frobenius norm.\n\n**Inputs:** an initial point $x_0\\in U$; either a positive fixed-step\nsequence $(t_k)_{k\\ge0}$ or backtracking parameters $\\alpha\\in(0,1)$ and\n$\\beta\\in(0,1)$; and an optional caller stopping predicate\n$S(k,x,g)\\in\\{\\mathrm{false},\\mathrm{true}\\}$, with\n$S:\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}\\times U\\times\\mathbb R^d\\to\\{\\mathrm{false},\\mathrm{true}\\}$.\nIf no predicate is supplied, use $S\\equiv\\mathrm{false}$. **Output:** when the\nprocedure stops, return the last valid iterate $x_k$ and the status\n`stationary`, `caller-stop`, or `domain-failure`.\nIf no stopping status occurs, the sequence continues and there is no finite\nreturn.\nFor example, an outer procedure's at-most-$M$-updates limit, for finite real\n$M\\ge0$, is implemented by $S(k,x,g):=(k+1>M)$.\n\nAt iteration $k$, where $k$ is the number of accepted updates so far and the\ncurrent iterate is $x_k$:\n\n1. Compute $g_k:=\\nabla f(x_k)$ and set $p_k:=-g_k$. If $g_k=0$, return\n   $(x_k,\\texttt{stationary})$.\n2. If $S(k,x_k,g_k)$ is true, return $(x_k,\\texttt{caller-stop})$. Check this\n   predicate at the current iterate after its gradient is computed and before\n   any step is selected.\n3. In fixed-step mode, set $t=t_k$. If $x_k+tp_k\\notin U$, return\n   $(x_k,\\texttt{domain-failure})$ without accepting that candidate.\n4. In backtracking mode, start with $t=1$ and repeatedly replace $t$ by\n   $\\beta t$ until $x_k+tp_k\\in U$ and\n   $f(x_k+tp_k)\\le f(x_k)+\\alpha t\\,\\langle g_k,p_k\\rangle$.\n5. Set $x_{k+1}:=x_k+t p_k$ and continue at iteration $k+1$.\n\nThe fixed-step update map is $x\\mapsto x-t_k\\nabla f(x)$; at a stationary\npoint its value is $x$, which is the returned iterate. A backtracking search\nthat is reached at a nonstationary iterate is well-defined by\nArmijo backtracking terminates for strict descent directions. The\nprocedure asserts no convergence or global-minimization property for an\narbitrary objective.",
